.TH KAFKA-INSPECT 1 "2026-10-19" "kafka-inspect" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kafka-inspect \- scan, describe, or produce to a Kafka topic (messages matching a key/header/field, size/metadata, or sending one message)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kafka-inspect
.B \-\-topic=\fIname\fR
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kafka-inspect
is a debugging/analysis CLI that answers "did this message land in this
topic, and what does it look like?" and "how big is this topic?" against a
real Kafka cluster. Every flag except
.B \-\-produce
is read-only: it never joins a consumer group and never commits offsets.
.B \-\-produce
is this tool's only write operation \- it sends exactly one message to a
topic and exits; it never auto-creates a missing topic (a hard error
instead), and every other flag is unaffected by its existence.
.PP
This page gives a short synopsis and a set of worked debugging examples.
For the authoritative, complete list of flags, their accepted values, and
the exact error each one produces on a bad input, run:
.PP
.RS
.B kafka-inspect \-\-help
.RE
.PP
A bare run with no config file anywhere still works and defaults to
scanning the 50 oldest messages on the given topic
(\fB\-\-limit=50 \-\-from=earliest\fR); every default is overridable via the
config file, a \fBSCANNER_*\fR environment variable, or a CLI flag, in that
order of precedence (CLI always wins).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
See what topics exist on the cluster
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-list\-topics
.EE
.RE
.TP
Check a topic's size before a destructive operation (message count, and best\-effort on\-disk bytes)
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-describe \-\-topic=orders
.EE
.RE
.TP
Learn a topic's shape with an unfiltered sample, oldest messages first
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-limit=50
.EE
.RE
.TP
Scan for a specific key/header/field value
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-match.field=status=ERROR
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-match.key=CUST\-106
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-match.header=source=mobile
.EE
.RE
.TP
Look at only the last hour, instead of from the beginning
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-from="$(date \-d '1 hour ago' '+%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S')" \-\-limit=50
.EE
.RE
.TP
Bound a scan to a specific time window (both ends)
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-from="2026\-10\-15 09:00:00" \-\-until="2026\-10\-15 10:00:00"
.EE
.RE
.TP
Tail a topic live, like \fItail \-f\fR, starting from now
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-from=latest \-\-follow
.EE
.RE
.TP
Jump straight to a known offset on one partition, instead of scanning for it
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-partition=0 \-\-from=offset:2309 \-\-limit=1
.EE
.RE
.TP
Quick cardinality check before handing off to Kibana/downstream aggregation
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-output=distinct \-\-distinct=header:source
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-output=distinct \-\-distinct=field:status \-\-match.field=status=ERROR
.EE
.RE
.TP
Pipe to \fIjq\fR for ad-hoc field inspection
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-output=json \-\-limit=20 | jq '.value.status'
.EE
.RE
.TP
Extract one message's exact original bytes (no decoding, no envelope)
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-topic=orders \-\-partition=0 \-\-from=offset:3801 \-\-limit=1 \-\-output=raw | xxd
.EE
.RE
.TP
Send a message to a topic (this tool's only write operation; fails if the topic doesn't exist)
.RS
.EX
kafka-inspect \-\-produce \-\-topic=orders \-\-key=order\-42 \-\-header=source=web \-\-value='{"id":42}'
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kafka-inspect/scanner.properties
Default config file on an RPM install (\fB%config(noreplace)\fR \- edits
survive an upgrade). A missing default config file is not an error; only an
explicitly given \fB\-\-config=\fR/\fBSCANNER_CONFIG\fR path that doesn't
exist is.
.TP
.I /etc/kafka-inspect/scanner.properties.example
The same template, fully commented \- every key documented. Installed
alongside for reference only; never read by \fBkafka-inspect\fR itself and
never edited in place (copy it over \fBscanner.properties\fR instead).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every scalar flag has a matching \fBSCANNER_*\fR override (e.g.
\fBSCANNER_TOPIC\fR, \fBSCANNER_FROM\fR, \fBSCANNER_UNTIL\fR,
\fBSCANNER_LIMIT\fR, \fBSCANNER_OUTPUT\fR, \fBSCANNER_FOLLOW\fR), plus the
open-ended \fBSCANNER_KAFKA_<NAME>\fR / \fBSCANNER_JVM_<NAME>\fR passthrough
namespaces for Kafka client properties and JVM system properties
respectively (dots become underscores, e.g.
\fBSCANNER_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS\fR). See \fBkafka-inspect \-\-help\fR for
the full set.
.TP
.B KAFKA_INSPECT_JAVA_HOME
Overrides the Java runtime the \fBkafka-inspect\fR wrapper script uses,
taking priority over \fB/etc/alternatives/java\fR, \fB$PATH\fR, and the
built-in fallback paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one match found.
.TP
.B 1
Ran cleanly, zero matches.
.TP
.B 2
Error (bad config/args, topic doesn't exist, broker unreachable, etc.).
.PP
.B \-\-produce
is the one exception to this table: it only ever exits 0 (sent) or 2
(error) \- there is no 1, since "zero matches" has no equivalent for a
write.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B kafka-inspect \-\-help
for the complete flag reference.
